---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Phone verification">
  <h1>Phone verification</h1>
  <p>
    A signed-in user proves they own a phone number with a 6-digit code sent
    to it. The number is stored on their account with
    <code>phone_verified</code> set. It is not a way to sign in: it is a fact
    about the account that a project can keep to one account per number and a
    tenant can require.
  </p>

  <h2 id="deployment">On the deployment</h2>
  <p>
    A code is sent over a <strong>channel</strong>, and the deployment decides
    which channels exist:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>sms</code> texts the code through the deployment's SMS provider:
      <code>GATEWAY_SMS_ENABLED=true</code>, <code>GATEWAY_SMS_PROVIDER</code>
      (<code>twilio</code>, <code>sns</code> or <code>azure</code>) and its
      credentials (<code>GATEWAY_SMS_*</code>); the server refuses to boot when
      the provider's are missing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>message</code> sends a pre-approved messaging-app template message
      through a <code>templatemsg.Sender</code>. This server ships no provider
      for it and has no environment variable for one: an embedder sets
      <code>identityserver.Options.TemplateMessageSender</code>. The template's
      one parameter is the code. A <code>Sender</code> must never log the
      code, nor put it in an error it returns.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="On the deployment">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>GATEWAY_PHONE_CODE_TTL_SECONDS</code></td><td><code>300</code></td><td>How long a code lasts.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GATEWAY_PHONE_CODE_MAX_ATTEMPTS</code></td><td><code>5</code></td><td>Guesses a code takes, the right one included, before it is spent.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GATEWAY_PHONE_CODE_COOLDOWN_SECONDS</code></td><td><code>60</code></td><td>The least time between two codes for one account.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="project">Per project</h2>
  <p>
    A project with no <code>phone</code> block in its <code>config_json</code>
    verifies phones as the deployment does, by SMS. The block can only narrow
    that: it can switch verification off, drop a channel and refuse calling
    codes, but cannot use a channel the deployment has no sender for.
  </p>
  <Code lang="json" code={`{
  "phone": {
    "unique": true,
    "reassign_after_seconds": 31536000,
    "allowed_calling_codes": ["1", "44"],
    "channels": ["message", "sms"],
    "message_template": "verification_code",
    "message_language": "en"
  }
}`} />
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Per project">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>enabled</code></td>
        <td>unset (follows the deployment)</td>
        <td><code>false</code> turns verification off for the project. <code>true</code> changes nothing: a project cannot verify phones without a sender.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>unique</code></td>
        <td><code>false</code></td>
        <td>A verified number belongs to one account of the project.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>reassign_after_seconds</code></td>
        <td><code>0</code> (never; at most <code>315360000</code>, ten years)</td>
        <td>With <code>unique</code>: how long after its holder verified a number another account that proves it may take it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>allowed_calling_codes</code></td>
        <td><code>[]</code> (every code)</td>
        <td>Country calling codes accepted, as 1 to 3 digits without the <code>+</code>. One code can cover several countries (<code>1</code> covers the whole North American numbering plan), so this narrows by calling code, not by country.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>channels</code></td>
        <td><code>[]</code> (<code>["sms"]</code>)</td>
        <td>The delivery order: <code>"sms"</code>, <code>"message"</code>. A channel left out is never used.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>message_template</code></td>
        <td><code>""</code></td>
        <td>The template the message channel sends, as registered with the provider. Required when <code>channels</code> lists <code>"message"</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>message_language</code></td>
        <td><code>""</code></td>
        <td>The template's language code, when the provider needs one.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The channels are tried in order and the first that accepts the message
    wins; one that fails is logged and the next is tried. A project switched
    off, and one none of whose channels has a sender here, both answer
    <code>UNAVAILABLE</code> with <code>phone verification is not
    available</code>. The log tells them apart:
    <code>phone_verification_unavailable</code> with
    <code>reason: switched_off_by_project</code>, or, as a warning, with
    <code>reason: no_channel_has_a_sender</code>, a misconfiguration every
    request to that project fails on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rpcs">Verifying and removing a number</h2>
  <Code lang="bash" code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/RequestPhoneVerification \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"phoneNumber":"+14155550123"}'

curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/VerifyPhoneCode \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"phoneNumber":"+14155550123","code":"123456"}'`} />
  <p>
    <code>RequestPhoneVerification</code> sends a code to an E.164 number.
    <code>VerifyPhoneCode</code> checks it against the number it was sent to
    and stores the number, verified. A number another account holds is sent a
    code like any other: whether a number is taken is told only to a caller
    whose code was right.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Verifying and removing a number">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Answer</th><th scope="col">When</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>UNAVAILABLE</code></td><td>The project cannot verify phones, or no channel delivered the code.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code></td><td>The number is not E.164.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>, <code>phone_country_not_allowed</code></td><td>The number is outside <code>allowed_calling_codes</code>; nothing is sent.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>, <code>recovery_hold</code></td><td>The session is held by a <a href="/identity/docs/auth/recovery-emails#the-hold">recovery hold</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code></td><td>An anonymous account (upgrade it first), or a child account when <code>GATEWAY_MINOR_DATA_MINIMIZATION</code> is on.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ALREADY_EXISTS</code></td><td><code>RequestPhoneVerification</code> for the number the account has already verified.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>UNAUTHENTICATED</code></td><td>A missing, expired, used up or wrong code, or one sent to another number.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ALREADY_EXISTS</code>, <code>phone_number_taken</code></td><td><code>VerifyPhoneCode</code> with a right code for a number another account holds, where the project keeps numbers unique.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every guess, right or wrong, counts against the code's attempt cap in the
    same write that reads the code, so guesses sent at once cannot pass it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>RemovePhone</code> clears the caller's number and its verified state
    and returns the user; a caller with no number gets their user back
    unchanged. It works whether or not the project verifies phones, mails the
    account's addresses, audits <code>phone_removed</code>
    (<code>reason: removed_by_user</code>), and is refused during a recovery
    hold like a change of number. It spends the per-IP budget
    <code>RequestPhoneVerification</code> does
    (<code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_PHONE_PER_IP</code>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="unique">One account per number</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>unique</code>, a verified number belongs to one account. It is
    free again when its holder removes it, verifies another number, or is
    deleted. With <code>reassign_after_seconds</code>, a number also moves to
    another account that proves it once that long has passed since its holder
    verified it, since carriers give a number its owner gave up to someone
    else: the previous holder's phone is cleared, <code>phone_removed</code>
    is audited for them (<code>reason: verified_by_another_account</code>),
    and their addresses are mailed. Numbers verified before the project turned
    <code>unique</code> on are not claimed: an older account keeps such a
    number, and it is held to one account from the next time it is verified.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <a href="/identity/docs/users/merging-accounts">merge</a> moves the
    retired account's number to a survivor that has none, and otherwise
    releases it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tenant-rule">A tenant that requires a verified phone</h2>
  <p>
    A tenant's login policy can require one:
    <code>LoginPolicy.require_verified_phone</code> makes
    <code>RefreshToken</code> refuse to select the tenant for an account with
    no verified phone, so no token for it is minted, and
    <code>phone_calling_codes</code> narrows the rule to numbers under those
    calling codes. Each entry is 1 to 3 digits, not starting with 0, listed
    once; anything else, or codes without the requirement, is
    <code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code>.
  </p>
  <Code lang="bash" code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/UpsertLoginPolicy \\
  -H "X-Admin-Secret: $GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"projectId":"<project>","tenantId":"<tenant>","requireVerifiedPhone":true,"phoneCallingCodes":["1","44"]}'`} />
  <p>
    The refusal is <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> with the stable
    <code>phone_verification_required</code> token and
    <code>TenantSelectionRefusedDetails.reason =
    TENANT_SELECTION_REFUSAL_PHONE_VERIFICATION_REQUIRED</code>, and leaves the
    refresh token unspent. It is not a sign-in rule: the account keeps signing
    in, refreshes without <code>tenant_id</code>, verifies a phone with that
    session, and selects the tenant again. It is checked at every selection,
    so removing the phone ends access at the next refresh. A session the
    policy would refuse for another reason is refused for that first.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rule is saved whatever the project can do. In a project that cannot
    verify phones (switched off, or with no sender), an account with no
    verified phone can never get one, so every such selection is refused:
    check the project before you require a phone.
  </p>
  <p>
    See <a href="/identity/docs/concepts/multi-tenancy">Multi-Tenancy</a> for
    tenant selection.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audit">Audit events</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>phone_verification_requested</code>: a code was sent.</li>
    <li><code>phone_verified</code>: a number was verified and stored.</li>
    <li><code>phone_removed</code>: a number left an account, with <code>reason</code> <code>removed_by_user</code> or <code>verified_by_another_account</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
